  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLow pressure system will pass further east and have removed\nalmost all the rain chances from our area for today and tonight.\nThere is a very small chance for a brief light rain shower in\nthe eastern Poconos or Catskills. Cold front pushes through the\nregion tomorrow with a low chance for an isolated shower. Behind\nthe front, cold air advection on NW flow will produce a weak\nlake effect response. This will bring a period of lake effect\nsnow showers to central NY Monday night through Tuesday morning,\nbut any accumulations will likely be only a dusting to less \nthan an inch. High pressure builds in Tuesday afternoon and this\nwill keep the area dry until at least Thanksgiving morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nNot much has changed with the Thanksgiving into Black Friday\nsystem. The current consensus is for a wet, and warm storm track\nfor our region with the low taking a more inland track, rather\nthan staying along the coast. The trough digging into the \ncentral US does look like it will try and become negatively \ntilted, so even though the low is taking the western, more \ninland track, it still looks like it could deepen quite a bit. \nWarm air wraps into the low from the south and east, with the \ndominant precipitation type being almost all rain. Although the\nsystem will be mostly rain across the area, there is a chance\nfor colder air to wrap around the system after it passes, so\ncould see a little wet snow or rain snow mix on Friday into\nFriday night. \n\nOne thing to watch may be the winds as the storm deepens. \nModels are hinting at a strong warm conveyer into the low on the\nnorth side with 850 mb winds over 50 knots. Depending on \nstability, there could be some stronger winds to watch out for \nThursday.",
